From: Lajoshazi, Laszlo Gergely <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] camera: one focus mode control, on the camera page

Focus was spread over two settings that could contradict each other and a
control the user could not find. An Autofocus switch chose between the
camera hunting and focusing on demand; a Focus Mode dropdown offered
"manual", which was wired to nothing at all - apply_focus_mode() acted only
on "face"; and the lens-distance slider was reachable only by finding a
switch called "Automatic - Focus Distance" in the preferences, which is not
where anyone looks while taking a picture.

Replace all of it with one setting, focus-mode, with four values:

  continuous  the camera keeps the whole picture in focus by itself
  face        the same, while following a face it has found
  tap         the camera does not hunt; a tap focuses and takes the picture
  off         nothing moves the lens but the focus-distance slider

and put it on the camera page, in a popover on a button next to the flash,
together with that slider. Focus is a control, not a preference: it is
changed while looking at the picture it changes.

A tap now takes the picture on its own in tap mode, rather than one tap
focusing and two focusing and shooting. The second press of a double tap is
swallowed there, since GestureClick reports n_press 1 and then 2 for one
gesture and both would fire the shutter. Double-tap-to-zoom stays in the
modes where the camera focuses for itself.

Focusing before a picture moves into take_picture(), so a tap and the
shutter button cannot drift apart: it focuses when the flash has just
changed the light, or when nothing has been keeping the picture in focus,
and never in off mode, where the distance is the user's.

Two waits were measured rather than assumed. FOCUS_SETTLE_MS and
CAPTURE_FOCUS_SETTLE_MS were both 900 ms against a full contrast sweep that
takes about 4.8 s on this hardware, read off the software ISP's own log:
twelve coarse positions, seven fine ones and two revisits at about 250 ms
each. The shutter therefore fired with the lens somewhere in the middle of
the sweep, which is worse than not focusing at all, an arbitrary sweep
position being worse than the previous focus. Both become 5.5 s. Making the
sweep shorter is a separate exercise.

Focusing on demand also asks for AfMeteringWindows, so that the score is
taken over the middle of the frame rather than all of it: a close subject's
sharpening is otherwise diluted by everything behind it that never
sharpens - measured at 4.9 % modulation against an 8 % minimum-contrast
gate, so the scan was rejected and the lens went back where it started.
Aiming that window at the tapped point is not possible from here: PipeWire's
libcamera plugin maps only scalar controls, so AfWindows never reaches the
camera.

Signed-off-by: Lajoshazi, Laszlo Gergely <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
